    "lyrics": "(Verse 1 — The Launch)\n\nAustin woke up gold on the third of September\nNo livestream, just the future rolling in\nForty-five machines with no hands on a wheel\nButterfly doors lifting like a storm about to begin\nTesla said the future arrived in Austin, TEXAS\nFirst car built from the ground for unsupervised drive\nPhone in your pocket and the door knows your name\nStep in, buckle up, tap Start Ride — you’re alive  \n\n(Chorus)\n\nA storm of TESLA Cybercabs, gold across the sky\nNo steering wheel stirring, no driver in sight\nA storm of Cybercabs, coming down the line\nFirst-class lounge on wheels at a coach-class price\nA storm of Cybercabs, flooding every street\nCamera eyes wide open, no LiDAR they need\nA storm of Cybercabs — the people’s cab is here\nSafer, cleaner, louder, the golden era is near  \n\n(Verse 2 — The Machine)\n\nTwo seats? three? that’s all you need, lounge instead of a cockpit\nNo pedals, no mirrors, no glove ..... box, hood stays locked\nMatte gold body, light-bars that change color when they find you\n22-inch theater screen running Unreal, music, games, and Grok\n\n\nEight cameras seeing everything, neural net calling the shots\nFront-wheel drive, 219 horses, motor with rare earths\n\nForty-eight kilowatt-hours, almost three hundred miles\nLightest Tesla ever — three thousand one hundred thirteen pounds\nWireless charge, no port, roll into the hub and drink\nAutomated clean between rides so the next guest finds it neat  \n\n(Chorus)\nA storm of Cybercabs, gold across the sky\nNo steering wheel stirring, no driver in sight\nA storm of Cybercabs, coming down the line\nFirst-class lounge on wheels at a coach-class price  \n\n(Verse 3 — Inside the Ride)\nForty-three point four inches of legroom, stretch out and breathe\nBench at wheelchair height, Braille on the buttons, service dog at your feet\nTrunk swallows two checked bags and two carry-ons easy\nTwo-twenty pounds, stroller, wheelchair — twenty cubic feet\n\nDoors rise like wings when your phone gets close\nClose themselves when the belts click — auto-tighten, no slack\nClimate and seats remember your profile from the last ride\nUSB-C in the armrest, independent recline on each side\n\nStop button between the dome lights if you need the world to pause\nMechanical release if danger’s real — it pulls over or holds the lane\nCabin camera checks you’re safe, then hunts for lost keys and crumbs\nRadar knows who’s sitting so the airbags know exactly what to do  \n\n(Bridge — Safety & Smarts)\nEngineered to be the safest car they ever put on the road\nDetects sirens, emergency lights, first-responder hands in the air\nExternal mics and speakers so support can talk without opening a door\n\nYields, pulls over, follows cones — Level 4 inside the geofence\nDynamic pricing rising with demand\nBut they promised first-class feeling at a coach-class fare\nStarlink V5 coming soon — 4K video even where the cell towers fail\nMore time back, more life back, the streets won’t be the same  \n\n(Verse 4 — The Storm Grows)\nProduction born at Giga Texas, cycle times chasing five to ten seconds\nMotor eighteen percent smaller, twenty-five percent lighter, still the range\nEfficiency that drinks almost nothing — urban miles on a whisper\n\nChina will see it mid-September, displays in Beijing and Shanghai\nNot for sale there yet — just the shape of what’s coming\nFleet buyers can already raise a hand\nDemocratizing clean transport, the people’s cab on every corner\nA lounge on wheels with a great TV and epic sound\nThat’s what Elon said while the gold dust settled on Austin  \n\n(Final Chorus — Bigger)\nA storm of Cybercabs, gold across the sky\nNo steering wheel stirring, no driver in sight\nA storm of Cybercabs, coming down the line\n\nFirst-class lounge on wheels at a coach-class price\nA storm of Cybercabs, flooding every street\nCamera eyes wide open, no LiDAR they need\nA storm of Cybercabs — the people’s cab is here\nSafer, cleaner, louder — the golden era is here  \n\n(Outro)\n’Twas the night before Cybercab\nNot a steering wheel stirring\nNor a driver in sight\nNow the storm is on the road\nAnd the future just arrived.  \n\nCan you hear me?\nCan you see me?\nCan you feel me?"
